 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Sort Order:** Splats must be processed from nearest to farthest. If processed far-to-near without adjusting the compositing math, occlusion inverts, causing far splats to wrongly paint over near ones [S1, unofficial].
-- **Sort-Order Flips (Popping Artifacts):** 3DGS sorts splats by their center depth $Z_c$. Since splats are 3D blobs with extent, overlapping splats can swap order as the camera moves, causing flickering. This is a speed/quality trade-off [S1, unofficial].
-- **Precision:** The transmittance $T$ decays exponentially. If $T$ becomes very small, floating-point precision issues may arise, though early termination mitigates this [G].
-- **Tiling Optimization:** For large images, split the image (e.g., 128×128) into tiles (e.g., 16×16) and compute which splats' ellipses overlap each tile using a cheap bounding-box test on center ± a few standard deviations [S1, unofficial].
-- **Worked Example:** Compositing three splats (red $\alpha=0.5$, green $\alpha=0.6$, blue $\alpha=0.8$) at a single pixel yields $\mathbf{C} \approx (0.5, 0.30, 0.16)$ with leftover transmittance $T = 0.04$ [S1, unofficial].
+- **Sort-Order Flips:** 3DGS sorts splats by center depth $Z_c$. Since splats are 3D blobs with extent, overlapping splats can swap order as the camera moves, causing flickering ("popping artifacts") [S1, unofficial]. This is a speed/quality trade-off [S1, unofficial].
+- **Incorrect Order:** If splats are processed far-to-near without adjusting the compositing math, occlusion inverts, causing far splats to wrongly paint over near ones [S1, unofficial].
+- **Precision:** The transmittance $T$ decays exponentially. Using a threshold $\epsilon$ prevents unnecessary computation for deeply occluded pixels [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Vectorized Front-to-Back Compositing](4305-vectorized-front-to-back-compositing.md)
-A Python/NumPy implementation that processes all pixels simultaneously using vectorized operations, trading computational efficiency for code simplicity and gradient stability.
+A Python/NumPy implementation of front-to-back alpha compositing that processes all pixels in an image simultaneously using vectorized operations, rather than per-pixel loops. Unlike GPU implementations that can perform per-pixel early termination, this NumPy version processes all splats for all pixels regardless of opacity, trading computational efficiency for code simplicity and gradient stability.
+
+### GEMM-based Alpha Compositing
+A method that maps alpha-blending and sorting operations to a sequence of General Matrix Multiplications (GEMMs). The per-tile blending becomes a matrix multiplication $C = AB$ where $A$ contains the Gaussian attributes and $B$ the pixel-wise accumulation weights [S3, unofficial].
+
+### Back-to-Front Grid Patch Rendering and Compositing
+The process of rendering ordered grid patches using standard DVR algorithms and compositing them into a final image. Patches are rendered as RGB images with an alpha channel and composited using an 'over' operation, either in software or in hardware as an alpha-blending operation [S4, unofficial]. In texture-mapping based hardware-assisted DVR algorithms, compositing is performed implicitly by rendering all grid patches into the same frame buffer [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$ in the worst case, where $N$ is the number of splats and $P$ is the number of pixels. However, with early termination, the average case is significantly faster, especially in dense scenes where pixels become opaque quickly [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(P)$ for storing the accumulated color and transmittance per pixel [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Early termination provides a significant speedup, often stopping most pixels after only a handful of contributions [S1, unofficial]. Tiling further optimizes this by reducing the number of splats considered per pixel (most tiles touch only 2–4 splats) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Correct occlusion handling, efficient early termination, simple algebra.
-- **Limitations:** Sort-order flips can cause visual artifacts (popping) [S1, unofficial]. Vectorized implementations may process all splats for all pixels, losing the early termination benefit [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$ in the worst case, where $N$ is the number of splats and $P$ is the number of pixels. However, with early termination, the average case is significantly faster, often processing only 2–4 splats per pixel in dense scenes [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store splat attributes and sorted lists [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** The early termination optimization provides a significant speedup, especially for dense scenes where pixels become fully opaque quickly [S1, unofficial]. Tiling further optimizes this by reducing the number of splats considered per pixel [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Correct occlusion handling, efficient early termination, simple algebraic formulation [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Sort-order flips can cause visual artifacts (popping) [S1, unofficial]. Requires sorting, which can be expensive if not optimized (e.g., via tiling or GPU primitives) [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to Use:** When correct occlusion is required and the scene has varying density, allowing early termination to save computation [S1, unofficial].
+- **When Not to Use:** When sort-order artifacts are unacceptable and more complex depth handling is needed, or when hardware acceleration for sorting is unavailable [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Front-to-Back Alpha Compositing is based on the "over" operator from Porter-Duff compositing, widely used in image editing (e.g., Photoshop) and CGI [S1, unofficial]. It is a fundamental building block in volume rendering and neural rendering methods like 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) [S1, unofficial]. The method was popularized in the context of 3DGS by Kerbl et al. (2023), though the underlying compositing math is standard in computer graphics [S1, unofficial].
+Front-to-back alpha compositing is a fundamental technique in computer graphics, originally derived from the Porter-Duff "over" operator used in image compositing [S1, unofficial]. It is widely used in Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) to render 2D Gaussian splats into a 2D image [S1, S2, unofficial]. The method is also analogous to ray marching in Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and traditional volume rendering, where transmittance is accumulated along a ray [S1, unofficial]. The specific application to 3DGS leverages the differentiability of the Gaussian functions to allow gradient-based optimization of scene parameters [S1, unofficial].
 
